"How to use Time Limits - Opal Help Center (https://help.opalapp.com/article/how-to-use-time-limits)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://help.opalapp.com/article/how-to-use-time-limits\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 124\nL0: cite0†✦What's new in Opal?Read release notes→†community.opalapp.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Legacy Opal Opal\nL3: \nL4: cite2†← All help articles L5: \nL6: How to use Opal’s features\nL7: # ⌛ How to use Time Limits\nL8: \nL9: Time Limits are an integral feature of Opal. They allow you to block apps/websites you find distracting for a scheduled period of time. All Opal users, regardless of subscription status, may set up three Rules (including Time Limits). Meanwhile, those cite3†who’ve upgraded to Opal Pro†opal.so may set unlimited Rules (on a weekly schedule).\nL10: \nL11: cite4†Image L12: ## How do Time Limits Work on iOS?\nL13: \nL14: When you reach your cumulative limit on the apps/websites you've selected,** **they will be blocked for a duration you set. That means if you set a 30-minute Time Limit which applies to both Instagram and TikTok, you may use that total on both or either before you are blocked. If you'd prefer to have 30 minutes for each app, you will need to set up individual Time Limits.\nL15: It’s very important to mention that due to Apple restrictions, Time Limits are affected by iOS’s “Share Across Devices” setting. If “Share Across Devices” is toggled on, Apple will combine Time Limit usage from every Apple device signed in to your Apple Account. This means that if you’re in a cite5†Family Sharing†www.apple.com group, your Time Limit may include usage reported on those devices too - even if your family members do not use Opal.\nL16: To prevent your Time Limits from including Screen Time from your Family Sharing group, follow these steps:\nL17:   * Open your device “Settings”\nL18:   * Tap on “Screen Time”\nL19:   * Toggle “Share Across Devices” to Off\nL20: \nL21: Unfortunately, Opal is unable to segregate this usage due to how Apple reports app Screen Time totals. Instead, you’ll need to ensure that “Share Across Devices” is toggled to Off to ensure that your Time Limits work as intended.\nL22: > ‼️ Toggling “Share Across Limits” to off in your device Settings will not remove Time Limit usage that has already been counted today. Instead, once that feature has been toggled off, your Time Limits will count only your individual usage moving forward.\nL23: ## How do I add a Time Limit on the iOS version?\nL24: \nL25: To add an Time Limit on the iOS version of the app, tap on the \"+ New Rule\" button on the \"My Apps\" tab and tap “Time Limit\". You may then edit the following information about your Time Limit by tapping on the adjacent editing icons:\nL26: \nL27:   * “When I use this App”\nL28:   * “For this long”\nL29:   * \"On these days\"\nL30:   * “Then block app until”\nL31:   * \"Disable Resets”\nL32: \nL33: In the \"Disable Resets\" option, you will have two protection level options available:\nL34:   * Yes: Take short breaks or cancel\nL35:   * No: (cite3†available only on Opal Pro†opal.so ): Can't take breaks or leave early. Get one emergency pass per week\nL36: \nL37: Once you’ve set up your Time Limit, tap “Hold to Commit” until you see the word “Committed” appear. Your Time Limit is now live.\nL38: ## How can I unblock my app or website in a Time Limit on the iOS version?\nL39: \nL40: You may only take breaks on apps or websites with “unblocks allowed.” If you have multiple Rules applying to the same app or website, “no unblocks allowed” will supersede all other protection levels. However, if unblocks are allowed, you may unblock by following these steps:\nL41:   * From the “My Apps” tab, tap on the blocked app you’d like to access\nL42:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL43:   * Tap “Continue”\nL44:   * Select how long you would like to “Unblock for….”\nL45:   * Tap “Unblock”\nL46: \nL47: If you’ve setup your Time Limit so that it does not allow unblocks, you will be unable to follow the steps above. Instead, you must wait until it resets to edit it.\nL48: ## How Can I Pause, Edit, or Delete My Time Limit on the iOS version?\nL49: \nL50: You may pause, edit, or delete Time Limits that have “resets” allowed. You may edit, cancel, or delete your Time Limit by following these steps:\nL51: \nL52:   * From the “My Apps\" tab, tap on your Time Limit (below “Rules”)\nL53: \nL54:   * Tap “Edit,” “Reset Limit,” or “Pause Rule” (depending on your preference)\nL55: \nL56:   * To “Edit” your Time Limit\nL57: \nL58:   * Tap “Edit”\nL59: \nL60:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL61: \nL62:   * Tap “Continue”\nL63:   * Make the changes you prefer, and tap “Done”\nL64: \nL65:   * If you instead want to “Delete Rule,” tap the button\nL66: \nL67:   * To “Reset Limit” on your Time Limit\nL68: \nL69:   * Tap “Reset Limit”\nL70: \nL71:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL72: \nL73:   * Tap “Continue”\nL74: \nL75:   * If you reset your Time Limit\nL76: \nL77:   * To “Pause Rule” on your Time Limit\nL78: \nL79:   * Tap “Pause Rule”\nL80: \nL81:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL82: \nL83:   * Tap “Continue”\nL84: \nL85:   * “Set pause duration”\nL86: \nL87:   * Tap “Pause”\nL88: \nL89:   * If you instead want to “Delete Rule,” tap the button\nL90: ## What if I need to access to an app or website that’s locked on the iOS version?\nL91: \nL92: If you require emergency access to an app that is locked, the iOS version of Opal has an Emergency Pass that you may once per week. Here's how to disable a locked app/website in the event of an emergency:\nL93:   * Open the Opal app on your iOS device\nL94:   * Tap on “Home”\nL95:   * Tap on “Profile” (accessible from the profile icon in the top right corner)\nL96:   * Tap on “Settings” (accessible from the gear icon in the top right corner)\nL97:   * Scroll down and tap on “Emergency Pass”\nL98:   * Tap “Hold to Redeem”\nL99: This button will cancel all of your currently running Sessions, Schedules, Time Limits, and/or Open Limits for one hour, and will also cancel any “Hard Mode” cite6†Timer that is active. If your recurrent Sessions, Schedules, Time Limits, and/or Open Limits are programmed to reinitiate, you must edit or delete them (as needed) immediately after using your Emergency Pass. It's also important to note that the \"Emergency Pass\" should only be used when necessary, as it is strictly available once per week.\nL100: If you've already used your “Emergency Pass” this week, our Support team is unable to force end a Session, Schedule, Time Limit, Open Limit, or Timer for you. For that reason, we recommend only using your Emergency Pass in a true emergency.\nL101: > ‼️ If you’re encountering issues with your Time Limit, please contact Support by navigating to your Settings > Contact Support.\nL102: \nL103: Does your app look different? You may be on an older version of Opal. Visit the cite1†Legacy Opal Help Center .\nL104: \nL105: © 2026 Opal OS Corporation. All rights reserved.\nL106: \nL107: Product\nL108: \nL109: cite7†Free Trial†opalapp.com cite7†Pricing†opalapp.com cite8†Features†opalapp.com cite9†Download for iOS†apps.apple.com cite10†Download for Mac†opal.so cite11†Download for Android†opalapp.com L110: \nL111: About\nL112: cite12†Scrolling Kills†scrollingkills.us cite13†Our Story†opalapp.com cite14†Careers†opalapp.com cite15†Merch Store†shop.opal.so cite16†Brand Kit†brandkit.opal.so cite17†Contact Us†www.opal.so L113: \nL114: Learn\nL115: \nL116: cite18†How to Block Instagram†www.opal.so cite19†How to Block TikTok†www.opal.so cite20†How to Block Twitter†www.opal.so cite21†How to Block Facebook†www.opal.so cite22†How to use Shortcuts†www.opal.so cite23†Screen Time†opalapp.com L117: \nL118: Community\nL119: cite24†Community Forum†community.opal.so cite25†Blog†opalapp.com cite26†Screen Time Blogs†opalapp.com cite27†Focus Coaching†opalapp.com L120: \nL121: Support\nL122: \nL123: cite2†Help Centre cite1†Legacy Opal Help Center (before 2026) cite28†Terms of Use†opalapp.com cite28†Privacy Policy†opalapp.com cite29†For Students†opalapp.com cite30†Gift Cards†opalapp.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to use Open Limit - Opal Help Center (https://help.opalapp.com/article/how-to-use-open-limit)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://help.opalapp.com/article/how-to-use-open-limit\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 113\nL0: cite0†✦What's new in Opal?Read release notes→†community.opalapp.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Legacy Opal Opal\nL3: \nL4: cite2†← All help articles L5: \nL6: How to use Opal’s features\nL7: # 🔓 How to use Open Limit\nL8: \nL9: Open Limits are an integral feature of Opal. They allow you to specify the maximum number of times you may open an app or website daily. All Opal users, regardless of subscription status, may set up three Rules (including Open Limits). Meanwhile, those cite3†who’ve upgraded to Opal Pro†opal.so may set unlimited Rules (on a weekly schedule).\nL10: \nL11: cite4†Image L12: ## How do I add an Open Limit on the iOS version?\nL13: \nL14: To add an Open Limit on the iOS version of the app, tap on the \"+\" button on the \"My Apps\" tab and tap “Open Limit”. You may then edit the following information about your Open Limit by tapping on the adjacent editing icons:\nL15: \nL16:   * “App opens per day”\nL17:   * “For this long”\nL18:   * \"On these days\"\nL19:   * “For this app”\nL20:   * \"Disable Resets\"\nL21: \nL22: In the \"Disable Resets\" option, you will have two protection level options available:\nL23:   * Yes: Take short breaks or cancel.\nL24:   * No: (cite3†available only on Opal Pro†opal.so ): Can't take breaks or leave early. Get one emergency pass per week.\nL25: \nL26: Once you’ve set up your Open Limit, tap “Hold to Commit” until you see the word “Committed” appear. Your Open Limit is now live. Please note that our Open Limit feature will limit you to ten opens daily.\nL27: ## How can I unlock my Open Limit on the iOS version?\nL28: \nL29: To access your app or website that has an Open Limit applied to it, follow these steps:\nL30: \nL31:   * From the “My Apps” tab, tap on the blocked app you’d like to access\nL32: \nL33:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL34: \nL35:   * Tap “Continue”\nL36: \nL37:   * Tap “Unblock for __”\nL38: \nL39:   * Please note it’s intended behaviour that the length of time you may unblock an Open Limit is locked at the interval you have selected for your Open Limit\nL40: ## How can I edit, reset or delete my Open Limit on the iOS version?\nL41: \nL42: You may only edit, reset, or delete your Open Limits on apps or websites that you’ve already exceeded your daily limit on if the Open Limit rule has “resets allowed.” If resets are allowed, you may edit, reset, or delete your Open Limit by following these steps:\nL43: \nL44:   * From the “My Apps” tab, tap on your Open Limit (below “Rules”)\nL45: \nL46:   * Tap “Edit,” “Reset Limit,” or “Pause Rule” (depending on your preference)\nL47: \nL48:   * To “Edit” your Open Limit\nL49:   * Tap “Edit”\nL50: \nL51:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL52: \nL53:   * Tap “Continue”\nL54: \nL55:   * Make the changes you prefer, and tap “Done”\nL56: \nL57:   * If you instead want to “Delete Rule,” tap the button\nL58: \nL59:   * To “Reset Limit” on your Open Limit\nL60: \nL61:   * Tap “Reset Limit”\nL62: \nL63:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL64: \nL65:   * Tap “Continue”\nL66: \nL67:   * To “Pause Rule” on your Open Limit\nL68: \nL69:   * Tap “Pause Rule”\nL70: \nL71:   * Wait for the Waiting Room to countdown\nL72: \nL73:   * Tap “Continue”\nL74: \nL75:   * “Set pause duration”\nL76: \nL77:   * Tap “Pause”\nL78:   * If you instead want to “Delete Rule,” tap the button\nL79: \nL80: > ❗ If your Open Limit does not allow resets and you’ve already opened it for the first time today, you will be unable to edit it. This is intended behaviour. To edit or delete your Open Limit, you must do so tomorrow before you’ve opened it for the first time. Otherwise, if you need urgent access to apps/websites in this scenario, you must use your cite5†Emergency Pass. L81: ## What if I need Emergency access to an app/website with “no resets allowed” on the iOS version?\nL82: \nL83: If you require emergency access to an app that is locked, the iOS version of Opal has an Emergency Pass that you may once per week. Here's how to disable a locked app/website in the event of an emergency:\nL84:   * Open the Opal app on your iOS device\nL85:   * Tap on “Home”\nL86:   * Tap on “Profile” (accessible from the profile icon in the top right corner)\nL87:   * Tap on “Settings” (accessible from the gear icon in the top right corner)\nL88:   * Scroll down and tap on “Emergency Pass”\nL89:   * Tap “Hold to Redeem”\nL90: This button will cancel all of your currently running Sessions, Schedules, Time Limits, and/or Open Limits for one hour, and will also cancel any “Hard Mode” cite6†Timer that is active. If your recurrent Schedules, Time Limits, and/or Open Limits are programmed to reinitiate, you must edit or delete them (as needed) immediately after using your Emergency Pass. It's also important to note that the \"Emergency Pass\" should only be used when necessary, as it is strictly available once per week.\nL91: If you've already used your “Emergency Pass” this week, our Support team is unable to force end a Session, Schedule, Time Limit, Open Limit, or Timer for you. For that reason, we recommend only using your Emergency Pass in a true emergency.\nL92: Does your app look different? You may be on an older version of Opal. Visit the cite1†Legacy Opal Help Center .\nL93: \nL94: © 2026 Opal OS Corporation. All rights reserved.\nL95: \nL96: Product\nL97: \nL98: cite7†Free Trial†opalapp.com cite7†Pricing†opalapp.com cite8†Features†opalapp.com cite9†Download for iOS†apps.apple.com cite10†Download for Mac†opal.so cite11†Download for Android†opalapp.com L99: \nL100: About\nL101: cite12†Scrolling Kills†scrollingkills.us cite13†Our Story†opalapp.com cite14†Careers†opalapp.com cite15†Merch Store†shop.opal.so cite16†Brand Kit†brandkit.opal.so cite17†Contact Us†www.opal.so L102: \nL103: Learn\nL104: \nL105: cite18†How to Block Instagram†www.opal.so cite19†How to Block TikTok†www.opal.so cite20†How to Block Twitter†www.opal.so cite21†How to Block Facebook†www.opal.so cite22†How to use Shortcuts†www.opal.so cite23†Screen Time†opalapp.com L106: \nL107: Community\nL108: cite24†Community Forum†community.opal.so cite25†Blog†opalapp.com cite26†Screen Time Blogs†opalapp.com cite27†Focus Coaching†opalapp.com L109: \nL110: Support\nL111: \nL112: cite2†Help Centre cite1†Legacy Opal Help Center (before 2026) cite28†Terms of Use†opalapp.com cite28†Privacy Policy†opalapp.com cite29†For Students†opalapp.com cite30†Gift Cards†opalapp.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to use Emergency Pass - Opal Help Center (https://help.opalapp.com/article/how-to-use-emergency-pass)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://help.opalapp.com/article/how-to-use-emergency-pass\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 48\nL0: cite0†✦What's new in Opal?Read release notes→†community.opalapp.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Legacy Opal Opal\nL3: \nL4: cite2†← All help articles L5: \nL6: How to use Opal’s features\nL7: # 🚨 How to use Emergency Pass\nL8: \nL9: cite3†Image L10: \nL11: If you’re stuck in a cite4†Session , cite5†Schedule , cite6†Time Limit , cite7†Open Limit , or cite8†Timer that does not allow unblocks or resets and you need emergency access to apps or websites, please use your Emergency Pass. To access your Emergency Pass while in a Session, Schedule, Time Limit, or Open Limit, take these steps:\nL12:   * Tap on “Home”\nL13:   * Tap on “Profile” (accessible from the top right corner)\nL14:   * Tap on “Settings” (accessible from the top right corner)\nL15:   * Scroll down and tap on “Emergency Pass”\nL16:   * Tap and hold on “Hold to Redeem”\nL17: \nL18: Once you’ve enabled your Emergency Pass, all apps and websites will be unblocked for one hour. If your Rules are programmed to reinitiate, you must edit or delete them (as needed) immediately after using your Emergency Pass. Your Emergency Pass is available once per week.\nL19: > ❗ Please note that Opal’s Support team is unable to force end Rules on your behalf, so you must follow the instructions above. If you've already used your Weekly Emergency Pass, you'll need to wait until that Rule ends or until your Weekly Emergency Pass resets to edit it.\nL20: ## What if I need Emergency access to an app or website while “Hard Mode” has been activated on my Timer?\nL21: \nL22: If you require emergency access to an app or website and you’re currently in a “Hard Mode” Timer, you must use your weekly Emergency Pass. Here's how to disable that Timer:\nL23: \nL24:   * Tap the “X” icon that appears in the top left corner\nL25:   * Tap and “Hold to Use Pass”\nL26: > ❗ Please note that Opal’s Support team is unable to force end Timers on your behalf, so you must follow the instructions above. If you've already used your Weekly Emergency Pass, you'll need to wait until that Timer ends to edit it.\nL27: \nL28: Does your app look different? You may be on an older version of Opal. Visit the cite1†Legacy Opal Help Center .\nL29: \nL30: © 2026 Opal OS Corporation. All rights reserved.\nL31: \nL32: Product\nL33: cite9†Free Trial†opalapp.com cite9†Pricing†opalapp.com cite10†Features†opalapp.com cite11†Download for iOS†apps.apple.com cite12†Download for Mac†opal.so cite13†Download for Android†opalapp.com L34: \nL35: About\nL36: \nL37: cite14†Scrolling Kills†scrollingkills.us cite15†Our Story†opalapp.com cite16†Careers†opalapp.com cite17†Merch Store†shop.opal.so cite18†Brand Kit†brandkit.opal.so cite19†Contact Us†www.opal.so L38: \nL39: Learn\nL40: cite20†How to Block Instagram†www.opal.so cite21†How to Block TikTok†www.opal.so cite22†How to Block Twitter†www.opal.so cite23†How to Block Facebook†www.opal.so cite24†How to use Shortcuts†www.opal.so cite25†Screen Time†opalapp.com L41: \nL42: Community\nL43: \nL44: cite26†Community Forum†community.opal.so cite27†Blog†opalapp.com cite28†Screen Time Blogs†opalapp.com cite29†Focus Coaching†opalapp.com L45: \nL46: Support\nL47: cite2†Help Centre cite1†Legacy Opal Help Center (before 2026) cite30†Terms of Use†opalapp.com cite30†Privacy Policy†opalapp.com cite31†For Students†opalapp.com cite32†Gift Cards†opalapp.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat’s included in one sec pro? (https://tutorials.one-sec.app/en/articles/3036418)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://tutorials.one-sec.app/en/articles/3036418\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 69\nL0: cite0†Help & Tutorials L1: \nL2: cite1†Contact us†riedel.wtf L3: \nL4: How can we help?\nL5: \nL6: In this article\nL7: \nL8: cite2†Why did we increase the price of our lifetime subscription? L9: # What’s included in one sec pro?\nL10: \nL11: Edited Friday, June 19 2026\nL12: \nL13: one sec is free to use for everyone, but is limited to target one app:\nL14: \nL15: You can test for free if the principle behind one sec works for you as well. one sec’s approach is cite3†scientifically proven & peer-reviewed†www.pnas.org , it reduces app usage by 57% on average. On top of that, we have received more than 80'000 5-star ratings on the App Store.\nL16: If you would like to use one sec with multiple apps, it’s time to upgrade: on top of unlimited apps, you’ll get access to a long list of amazing features (see below!).\nL17:  |  | Free  | Pro (iOS)  | Pro (Android)  | Pro (Desktop Web Browser)\nL18: 💨  | Breathing Exercise  | 1 App  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅\nL19: 🔁  | cite4†Re-Intervention | 1 App  | ♾️  | ♾️  | ♾️\nL20: ♾️  | Unlimited Apps  | ❌  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅\nL21: 📱  | Different cite5†Intervention Types | ❌  | ✅  | N/A  | ✅\nL22: 🔄  | cite6†Intentional App Switching | ❌  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅\nL23: 🔔  | cite7†“Don‘t Get Lost” Notifications | ❌  | ✅  | N/A  | N/A\nL24: ⏱️  | Time Tracking  | ❌  | ✅  | ✅  | ✅\nL25: 🧭  | cite8†Website Blocking | ❌  | ✅  | N/A  | ✅\nL26: 🧠  | Strict cite9†Block Sessions | ❌  | ✅  | N/A  | N/A\nL27: 🍑  | Porn Blocker  | Soft  | 🔒 Strict  | N/A  | N/A\nL28: cite10†Get Free Trial →†web.one-sec.app L29: \nL30: > one sec is a so-called “indie-app”, built by a human (me, Frederik Riedel), not a corporation. I don’t have any investors who dilute my mission to build the best app to enable healthy relationships between humans and technology. Unlike many other apps, I make money selling apps and subscriptions, not your data. If you like that approach, please cite10†consider upgrading†web.one-sec.app to one sec pro.\nL31: ### Why did we increase the price of our lifetime subscription?\nL32: \nL33: one sec has grown a lot in terms of functionality, stability, and also as a team. And in general, unfortunately, everything has become more expensive in recent years.\nL34: \nL35: Back in 2021, one sec was iOS-only and just included the basic intervention mechanism, just like in the free version today.\nL36: Up to now, from cite5†various intervention types , cite4†Re-Intervention , the cite11†Phone Free Morning , cite12†scheduling , cite9†block sessions , cite13†Focus Filters , cite14†healthy alternatives , cite15†intention tracking , emotion tracking, integrations with cite16†Lengo and cite17†Structured , and the cite18†various lock cite19†functions , etc. – a ton of great tools have been added that weren’t factored into the original pricing. And there are more coming soon – we’re constantly working on it!\nL37: In parallel, we started developing cite20†browser extensions, a macOS version , and an cite21†Android version – all of which are included in every purchase, which helps fund the work done so far and the work still to come.\nL38: At the same time, cite22†our team†one-sec.app has grown. Compared to others, we’re still a small team.\nL39: But now we have Android, Browser Extension, MacOS, and iOS developers, researchers & psychologists, backend developers, people in marketing and customer support, and a UX/UI design team – all people working passionately on this app and getting paid for it.\nL40: Sure, most of us still work part-time or mini-jobs, but this tiny indie app has grown massively. And with it, the effort to keep developing and maintaining it. That’s constant work, as operating systems evolve – and so do the needs of our users, which you can see on our cite23†feedback board†feedback.one-sec.app and cite24†our roadmap†feedback.one-sec.app .\nL41: \nL42: We can only build a sustainable business long-term with subscriptions and recurring payments.\nL43: We believe our Pro subscriptions plans are still super affordable: for just $19 per year, a user saves an average of four weeks’ worth of time. We also offer a 50% discount for students.\nL44: \nL45: If that’s still too much money for you, just send us a message – we’ll definitely find a solution!\nL46: ## Was this article helpful?\nL47: \nL48: Yes\nL49: \nL50: No\nL51: \nL52: Sorry about that! Care to tell us more?\nL53: \nL54: What was unhelpful?\nL55: \nL56: Submit\nL57: \nL58: ## Thanks for the feedback!\nL59: \nL60: There was an issue submitting your feedback\nL61: 